@TITLE@ QilinRecomp for Windows (64-bit)
===============================================

@FULL_TITLE@ (@STUDIO@; Rhythm Heaven Fever in North America),
recompiled to run natively: the game's own program translated into C++ and
compiled for your PC, drawn with Vulkan.

No game data is included. You need your own copy of the game: @RELEASE@,
extracted into a folder with Dolphin. Other regions and revisions (Rhythm
Heaven Fever, Minna no Rhythm Tengoku) are other programs and aren't
supported.


Your game folder
----------------
In Dolphin, right-click the game in the list, choose Properties, open the
Filesystem tab, right-click the disc at the top and choose Extract Entire
Disc, and pick an empty folder. The folder you need is the one holding
"sys" and "files": if Dolphin made a DATA folder inside the one you picked,
it is that DATA folder. A .wbfs, .iso or .rvz file can't be read as it is:
extract it first.


Playing
-------
1. Unzip the download anywhere. Building the game needs about 2 GB free.
2. Open the folder and double-click "Play @TITLE@.exe".
3. Click "Choose your extracted disc" and pick the folder above, or drag it
   onto the window. It tells you straight away if it is the wrong game,
   another region or another revision, or if part of the disc is missing.
4. Wait for the first build: a few minutes. Your game files never leave
   your PC.
5. Click Play. The window hides while the game runs. "Add a desktop
   shortcut" puts Play @TITLE@ on your desktop.

After that, Play @TITLE@.exe remembers your folder (even in a new download
folder), and Play starts the game straight away. After an update it builds
the game again first, recompiling only what changed. Leave the disc folder
where it is: the game reads its files from it while you play.

The game's output is in work\run.log and work\run-errors.log, and the
build's in work\build.log. Send them when reporting a problem. If the
picture stays black after the first screen, close the game and start it
again (a rare fault at start-up, not yet found).

If Windows says "Windows protected your PC": More info, then Run anyway.
(The download isn't signed.) Or before unpacking, right-click the zip,
Properties, tick Unblock, OK.

Needs: Windows 10 or 11, 64-bit, and a graphics driver with Vulkan 1.3
(any recent NVIDIA, AMD or Intel driver).


If you used install.cmd before
------------------------------
Earlier versions were built from the source with install.cmd, which
installed the game into %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Beat the Beat - Rhythm
Paradise with its own shortcuts. This download doesn't touch that copy, so
you may have both. To remove the old one: Settings, Apps, Installed apps,
"Beat the Beat: Rhythm Paradise (Qilin recompilation)", Uninstall (or
Uninstall.cmd in that folder). Both use the same saves and settings
(below), and the uninstaller leaves them where they are.


Controls
--------
A Wii Remote held upright, buttons only (no motion controls).

  Controller               Keyboard                     Wii Remote
  south button (Xbox A),   A, Right Shift, Right        A
  right shoulder           arrow, Space
  east button (Xbox B),    B, Left Shift, Left arrow    B (the trigger)
  left shoulder, right
  trigger
  west, north buttons      1, 2                         1, 2
  Start, Back              Enter; Backspace or Tab      +; -
  Guide                    H                            HOME
  d-pad, left stick        I J K L, Up and Down arrows  the d-pad

On the title screen, press A and B together. Every connected controller
plays, and rumble reaches them.

F1 or Esc (a controller: Back and Start together): the settings menu (the
game pauses), with the controls (set by pressing them), the picture (size,
a CRT look, internal resolution, filtering, vsync) and the console's own
settings. Alt+Enter or F11: full screen. F3: frame times, the display's
refresh and the sound's lag.

Sound uses Windows' exclusive mode where the device allows it, for the
least lag; [audio] exclusive = false in the settings file shares the device
instead.

Settings (beatthebeat.ini), saves (nand) and the shader cache are kept in
%LOCALAPPDATA%\qilin\@GAME@, with the folder you chose and the build's cache
of compiled files (build-cache).


What's in here
--------------
Play @TITLE@.exe  chooses your extracted disc, builds the game (once) and starts it
kit\qilin.exe       the recompiler: turns the disc's @BOOT@ into C++
kit\rhf             the Rhythm Heaven Fever decompilation's function names,
                    which the recompiler matches to your disc's code
kit\lib, include    the prebuilt rest of the game: its runtime, the renderer,
                    sound, the window and controls (SDL3), the menu (Dear ImGui)
kit\toolchain       LLVM-MinGW, a C++ compiler for Windows (Clang)
kit\files.txt       every file this release holds (see below)
work\               made when building: the game (@EXE@), built from your
                    disc, the recompiled code, the build's files and the logs
LICENSES\           the licences of everything included

Source, changes and issues: https://github.com/@REPO@


Updates and recovery
--------------------
"Check for updates" in Play @TITLE@.exe asks GitHub for a newer release
(it sends nothing about you or your PC). If there is one, it downloads it,
unpacks it beside this folder and swaps in the new kit and launcher, then
starts again and builds the game for the new version. If anything fails on
the way, the old version is left as it was. You can also unzip a new
download anywhere, even over this folder: it finds your folder and the
cache by itself, and at its first start it removes files the new kit no
longer has (anything in kit\ or LICENSES\ that kit\files.txt doesn't name).
Your saves and settings are never in this folder, so nothing of yours is
touched.

The game is compiled for this PC's processor; moved to another PC, it is
built again. Compiled files come from the cache when nothing they're made
from has changed. A failed build leaves the working game as it was; the one
before a new build is kept as work\@EXE@.previous until the new one has run
and closed normally. "Build again from scratch" compiles everything anew.
